A||||3||thomas_l||Fixed my 87 headlight motors||||14||
Z||000000||thomas_l||05-27-2001||08:01 AM||||I got the bushing kit from Rodney Dickman and fixed my lift motors. In typical fashion as everything else, this job was also cobbled up by the $%^#^ that used to work on this car. When I first got this car I had taken the motor apart and cleaned & lubed the guts but never separated the driven gear (where the bushings go). I did notice that one of the cover screws was missing so I found one and replaced it. Well, since then, that light (RH) was really starting to act bad after working well for a while. When I took it apart yesterday, I found the missing cover screw. Bozo the shadetree had wedged it inside the driven gear in lieu of the correct bushings. Fortunately, the screw floating around in there didn't damage the parts too bad and the new bushings worked well. I did the LH motor too. It seemed like a reman unit that had been installed since it was in too good a condition. I did notice that the bushings in that one were a little more rounded and of a softer material. I am glad I opened it up and did it instead of waiting for it to break.||||reg||14
Z||000001||kslish||05-27-2001||06:20 PM||||I did this very same thing a few days ago on my '87GT (got the kit off of Rodney Dickman at Carlisle).  Piece of cake once I figured out where the bushings went (there was absolutely nothing left of the old ones)...and probably one of the cheapest repairs I've done to my Fiero.<P>Ken S.<BR>'87GT and '86SC<BR> <A HREF="http://www.westpennfiero.org" TARGET=_blank>www.westpennfiero.org</A> ||||reg||1
Z||000002||FieroJoe||05-27-2001||11:04 PM||||I wonder what he was thinking, to put that screw in there...<P>"Hmm, well it seems something is missing in here, maybe if I just throw parts at it, it will work!"<P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/joesig_1.jpg"> <BR>'87 Coupe 5spd.<BR>AIM:FieroJoe87||||reg||1
Z||000003||thomas_l||05-28-2001||08:55 AM||||Actually the idea wasn't that bad - just the choice of material. Since the objective was to lock the two parts of the driven gear together, substituting the nylon pieces with something else would have been ok in a pinch. I probably would have used some old throw-away sockets or carved some wood to fit in there. But this was probably done by the same guy that stripped one of the distributor cap screw holes and just left the cap loose.||||reg||1
